[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 \item Bit vectors  An $n$-bit vector or array can represent 
any subset $S$ on a universal set $U$ containing $n$ items. Bit $i$ is set to 1
 if $i \in S$, and 0 if not. Because only one bit is needed per element, bit ve
ctors can be very space efficient for surprisingly large values of $|U|$. Eleme
nt insertion and deletion simply flips the appropriate bit. Intersection and un
ion are done by "and-ing" or "or-ing" the bits together. The primary drawback o
f a bit vector is its performance on sparse subsets. For example, it takes $O(n
)$ time to explicitly identify all the members of a sparse (or even empty) subs
et $S$. 
 []

