[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 \item Bloom filters  We can emulate a bit vector in the abse
nce of a fixed universal set by hashing each subset element to an integer from 
0 to $n-1$ and setting the corresponding bit. Thus, bit $H(e)$ is set to 1 if $
e \in S$. Collisions leave some possibility for error under this scheme, howeve
r, because a different key might have hashed to the same position. A Bloom filt
er uses several (say $k$) different hash functions $H_{1}, \ldots H_{k}$, and s
ets all $k$ bits $H_{i}(e)$ upon insertion of key $e$. Now $e$ can be in $S$ on
ly if all $k$ bits are 1. The probability of false positives can be made arbitr
arily low by increasing the number of hash functions $k$ and the table size $n$
. This hashing-based data structure is much more space-efficient than dictionar
ies, for static subset applications that can tolerate a small probability of er
ror. Many can. For instance, spell checkers that occasionally leave some rare r
andom string uncorrected would prove no great tragedy. Bloom filters are more f
ully described in Section 6.4 
