Overfull \hbox (2195.99261pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 11392--11392
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 \textbf{Discussion}: Bandwidth reduction lurks as a hidden b
ut important problem for both graphs and matrices. Applied to matrices, bandwid
th reduction permutes the rows and columns of a sparse matrix to minimize the d
istance $b$ of the furthest non-zero entry from the center diagonal. This is im
portant in solving linear systems, because Gaussian elimination can be performe
d in $O(nb^{2})$ on matrices of bandwidth $b$: a big win over the general $O(n^
{3})$ algorithm if $b \ll n$. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2059.49301pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 11394--11394
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Bandwidth minimization on graphs arises in more subtle ways.
 Arranging $n$ circuit components in a row to minimize the length of the longes
t wire (and hence time delay) is a bandwidth problem, where each vertex of grap
h $G$ corresponds to a circuit component and there is an edge for every wire li
nking two components. More general formulations such as rectangular circuit lay
outs inherit the same hardness and classes of heuristics from the linear versio
ns. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2773.49094pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 11396--11396
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 The \emph{bandwidth} problem seeks a linear ordering of the 
vertices, which minimizes the length of the longest edge. But there are other v
ariations of the problem. In \emph{linear arrangement}, we seek to minimize the
 sum of the lengths of the edges. This has application to circuit layout, where
 we seek to position the chips to minimize the total wire length. In \emph{prof
ile minimization}, we seek to minimize the sum of one-way distances, for each v
ertex $v$ the length of the longest edge whose other vertex is to the left of $
v$.\index{Lagrangian relaxation}\index{Metropolis algorithm} 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1229.99542pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 11398--11398
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Unfortunately, all these variants of bandwidth minimization 
are NP-complete. It stays NP-complete even if the input graph is a tree whose m
aximum vertex degree is 3, which is about as strong a condition as I have seen 
on any problem. Therefore, our only options are brute-force search and heuristi
cs. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3314.23936pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 11400--11400
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Fortunately, ad hoc heuristics have been well studied and pr
oduction-quality implementations of the best heuristics are available. These ar
e based on performing a breadth-first search from a given vertex $v$, where $v$
 is placed at the left-most point of the ordering. All of the vertices that are
 distance 1 from $v$ are placed to its immediate right, followed by all the ver
tices at distance 2, and so forth until all vertices in $G$ are accounted for. 
The popular heuristics differ according to how many different start vertices ar
e considered, and how equidistant vertices are ordered among themselves. Breaki
ng ties with low-degree vertices over to the left generally seems to be a good 
idea. 
 []

