[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 These characterizations of Eulerian graphs make it is easy t
o test whether such a path/cycle exists: verify that the graph is connected usi
ng DFS or BFS, and then count the number of odd-degree vertices. The cycle can 
also be explicitly constructed in linear time by Hierholzers algorithm. Use DFS
 to find an arbitrary cycle in the graph. Delete this cycle and repeat, until t
he entire set of edges has been partitioned into a set of edge-disjoint cycles.
 Because deleting a cycle reduces the degree of each vertex by an even number, 
the remaining graph will continue to satisfy the Eulerian degree-bound conditio
ns. These cycles must have common vertices (because the graph is connected), an
d so can be spliced together in a figure eight at any shared vertex. By splicin
g all extracted cycles together, we get a single circuit containing all of the 
edges. 
