[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Trees are always planar graphs, and hence can and should be 
drawn so no two edges cross. Any of the planar drawing algorithms discussed in 
Section 18.12 (page 581) could be used to do so. But such algorithms are overki
ll, because there are much simpler ways of constructing planar drawings of tree
s. The spring-embedding heuristics of Section 18.10 (page 574) work well on fre
e trees, but might be too slow for interactive applications. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2138.24278pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 13279--13279
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 The most natural tree-drawing algorithms assume rooted trees
. However, they can be used equally well with free trees, after selecting one v
ertex to serve as the root of the drawing. This faux-root can be selected arbit
rarily, or, even better, by using a \emph{center} vertex of the tree. A center 
vertex minimizes the maximum distance to other vertices. This tree center can b
e identified in linear time, by repeatedly trimming all the leaves until only t
he center remains. 
 []


