[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 If you \emph{really} need to find the largest clique in a gr
aph, an exhaustive search via backtracking provides the only real solution. We 
search through all $k$-subsets of the vertices, pruning a subset as soon as it 
contains a vertex that is not adjacent to all the rest. A simple upper bound on
 the size of the maximum clique in $G$ is the highest vertex degree plus 1. A b
etter upper bound comes from sorting the vertices in order of decreasing degree
. Let $j$ be the largest index such that degree of vertex $v_{j}$ is at least $
j-1$. The largest clique in the graph contains no more than $j$ vertices, since
 no vertex of degree less than $(j-1)$ can appear in a clique of size $j$. To s
peed our search, first delete all such low degree vertices from $G$.\index{bran
ch-and-bound search}\index{distance graph} 
