raph isomorphism. A mapping of a graph to itself is called an \emph{automorphis
m}, and the collection of automorphisms (the automorphism \emph{group}) provide
s a great deal of information about symmetries in the graph. For example, the c
omplete graph $K_{n}$ has $n!$ automorphisms (because any mapping will do), whi
le an arbitrary random graph is likely to only have one, because $G$ is always 
identical to itself. 
