avor than packing squares into a rectangular box. In \textit{one-dimensional bi
n packing}, each object\TS1/lmtt/m/it/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 s size is given simp
ly as an integer. This is equivalent to packing boxes of equal width into a chi
mney of that width, and makes it a special case of the knapsack problem of Sect
ion 16.10 (page 497). 
