gnment. They often start by computing the pairwise alignment for each pair of s
trings. One approach replaces the two most similar sequences with a single merg
ed sequence, and repeats until all alignments have been merged into one. The ca
tch is that two strings often have many different alignments of optimal cost. T
he \TS1/lmtt/m/it/10 ``\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 right\TS1/lmtt/m/it/10 '' \T1/lmtt/m/it
/10 alignment to pick depends upon the subsequent sequences to merge, and hence
 unknowable to the heuristic. 
 []


