Overfull \hbox (353.24797pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15319--15319
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 \textbf{Problem description}: Find the shortest string $S^{\
prime}$ that contains each string $S_{i}$ as a substring of $S^{\prime}$. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2447.99188pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15321--15321
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 \textbf{Discussion}: Shortest common superstring/supersequen
ce (SCS) arises in a variety of applications. A casino gambling addict once ask
ed me how to reconstruct the pattern of symbols on the wheels of a slot machine
. With every spin, the wheel turns to a random position, displaying the selecte
d symbol as well as the symbols immediately before/after it. Given enough obser
vations of the slot machine, the symbol order for each wheel can be determined 
as the shortest common (circular) superstring of the observed symbol triples. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2789.24089pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15323--15323
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 A more important application of shortest common superstring 
is data/matrix compression. Suppose we are given a sparse $n \times m$ matrix $
M$, meaning that most elements are zero. We can partition each row into $m / k$
 runs of $k$ elements, and construct the shortest common superstring $S^{\prime
}$ of all these runs. We can now represent the matrix by the superstring, plus 
an $n \times m / k$ array of pointers denoting where each run starts in $S^{\pr
ime}$. Any particular element $M[i, j]$ can still be accessed in constant time,
 but there will be substantial space savings when $|S| \ll mn$. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1838.99365pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15325--15325
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Perhaps the most compelling application is in DNA sequence a
ssembly. Machines readily sequence fragments of 100 to 1,000 base pairs, or cha
racters of DNA. But the real interest is in sequencing large molecules. Large-s
cale "shotgun" sequencing clones many copies of the target molecule, breaks the
m randomly into fragments, sequences the fragments, and then proposes the short
est superstring as the correct sequence. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1014.74605pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15327--15327
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Finding a superstring of a set of strings is not difficult, 
since we can simply concatenate them together. Finding the \emph{shortest} such
 string is what\TS1/lmtt/m/it/10 '\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 s problematic. Indeed, short
est common superstring is NP-complete for all reasonable classes of strings. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (3062.2401pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15329--15329
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Finding the shortest common superstring can be reduced to th
e traveling salesman problem (see Section 19.4 (page 594)). Create a directed o
verlap graph $G$, where vertex $v_i$ represents string $S_i$. Assign edge $(v_i
, v_j)$ weight equal to the length of $S_i$ minus the overlap of $S_j$ with $S_
i$. Thus, $w(v_i, v_j)=1$ for $S_i=\textit{abc}$ and $S_j=\textit{bcd}$. The mi
nimum weight path visiting all the vertices defines the shortest common superst
ring. These edge weights are not symmetric: note that $w(v_j, v_i)=3$ for the e
xample above. Unfortunately, asymmetric TSP problems are much harder to solve i
n practice than symmetric instances. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (4259.23662pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15331--15331
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 The greedy heuristic provides the standard approach to appro
ximating shortest common superstring. Identify which pair of strings have the m
aximum overlap. Replace them by the merged string, and repeat until only one st
ring remains. This heuristic can be implemented in linear time. The seemingly m
ost time-consuming part is in building the overlap graph. The brute-force appro
ach to finding the maximum overlap of two length-$l$ strings takes $O(l^2)$ tim
e for each of $O(n^2)$ string pairs. But a faster construction is made possible
 by suffix trees (see Section 15.3 (page 448)). Build a tree containing all suf
fixes of all strings from $S$. String $S_i$ overlaps with $S_j$ iff a suffix of
 $S_i$ matches the prefix of $S_j$---an event defined by a vertex of the suffix
 tree. Traversing these vertices in order of distance from the root defines the
 appropriate merging order. 
 []


Overfull \hbox (2279.99237pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15333--15333
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 How well does the greedy heuristic perform? It can certainly
 be fooled into creating a superstring that is twice as long as optimal. The op
timal merging order for strings $c(ab)^k$, $(ba)^k$, and $(ab)^k c$ is left to 
right. But greedy starts by merging the first and third string, leaving the mid
dle one no overlap possibility. The greedy superstring can never be worse than 
3.5 times optimal, and usually will be much better in practice.\index{consultin
g services}\index{online algorithm resources} 
 []


Overfull \hbox (1723.49399pt too wide) in paragraph at lines 15335--15335
[]\T1/lmtt/m/it/10 Building superstrings becomes difficult when the input conta
ins both positive strings (each of which \emph{must} be a substring of the supe
rstring) and negative strings (each of which is \emph{forbidden} to appear in t
he final result). Deciding whether \emph{any} such consistent string exists is 
NP-complete, unless you are allowed to add an extra character to the alphabet t
o use as a spacer. 
 []


